 "script": [
  {
   "id": "hook",
   "text": "Israel's election has no clear winner. So who decides the next government?"
  },
  {
   "id": "f1",
   "text": "Exit polls on Tuesday night split. Channel 12 first showed Eisenkot's bloc ahead, fifty-two to fifty. A revised poll flipped it: fifty-two seats for Netanyahu's bloc, forty-nine for Eisenkot's. Kan showed a tie."
  },
  {
   "id": "f2",
   "text": "Likud is projected near twenty-seven seats, Eisenkot's Yashar about twenty-one. But neither bloc reaches sixty-one of one hundred twenty. Arab parties are projected at sixteen to nineteen seats. Netanyahu's side rules out working with them, and Eisenkot has all but ruled it out too."
  },
  {
   "id": "f3",
   "text": "Both men claimed victory. Netanyahu urged a strong right-wing government. These are exit polls, not official results. The Central Elections Committee is still counting. Axios says Netanyahu would likely stay on as caretaker prime minister."
  },
  {
   "id": "cta",
   "text": "Will Israel need yet another election? Yes or no?"
  }
 ],
